"viewpoint","la":-43.597386,"lo":172.705575,"d":1568},{"n":"Corsair Bay","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.607904,"lo":172.699676,"d":1729},{"n":"Upper Gondola Station","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.589479,"lo":172.716501,"d":1872},{"n":"Christchurch Discovery Ride","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.589444,"lo":172.716672,"d":1873},{"n":"Mount Pleasant World War Ⅱ 3.7″ Anti-aircraft Battery","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":-43.591726,"lo":172.735655,"d":1977},{"n":"Anti Aircraft Battery (Historic)","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":-43.59049,"lo":172.737805,"d":2192,"w":"https://sites.google.com/site/nzcoastaldefences/lyttelton-s-coastal-defences/anti-aircraft-battery-mt-pleasant"},{"n":"Port hills","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.58693,"lo":172.730423,"d":2252,"ds":"The Port Hills (Māori: Ngā Kōhatu Whakarakaraka o Tamatea Pōkai Whenua) are a range of hills in Canterbury Region of New Zealand, so named because they lie between the city of Christchurch and its port at Lyttelton. They are an eroded remnant of the Lyttelton volcano, which erupted millions of years ago.","wpu":"Port Hills","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/View_of_Port_Hills_from_Mt_Ada%2C_Canterbury%2C_New_Zealand.jpg/960px-View_of_Port_Hills_from_Mt_Ada%2C_Canterbury%2C_New_Zealand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cass Bay","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.60584,"lo":172.691092,"d":2408},{"n":"Godley House","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":-43.624646,"lo":172.740399,"d":2595},{"n":"Fort Jervois","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":-43.619837,"lo":172.754459,"d":3102},{"n":"Rīpapa","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":-43.620466,"lo":172.754547,"d":3143},{"n":"Shelly Cove","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.621964,"lo":172.75427,"d":3213},{"n":"Whakamaru Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.630565,"lo":172.694532,"d":3464},{"n":"Skiers Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.630722,"lo":172.691146,"d":3651},{"n":"War Memorial Seat","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":-43.593412,"lo":172.677805,"d":3749},{"n":"Ships' Graveyard","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.627988,"lo":172.684275,"d":3836},{"n":"Walkers Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.63169,"lo":172.687095,"d":3950,"wd":"Q125563669"},{"n":"Ferrymead Heritage Park","g":"sights","k":"theme_park","la":-43.567721,"lo":172.702907,"d":4498,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-16:30","w":"https://www.ferrymead.org.nz/","ph":"+64 3 384 1970","wd":"Q5445548","ad":"50 Ferrymead Park Drive"},{"n":"seat","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.595908,"lo":172.660353,"d":5010},{"n":"seat","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.590671,"lo":172.662076,"d":5041},{"n":"Matuku Takotako: Sumner Centre","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":-43.569105,"lo":172.758762,"d":5106,"h":"Mo-Fr 10:00-18:00; Sa 10:00-13:00","w":"https://my.christchurchcitylibraries.com/locations/sumner/","ph":"+64 3 941 7923","ad":"37 Nayland Street"},{"n":"Hidden Valley lookout","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.591873,"lo":172.659884,"d":5166},{"n":"Taylor's Mistake Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.583364,"lo":172.777078,"d":5170},{"n":"Sumner Coronation Garden","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.565809,"lo":172.755301,"d":5254},{"n":"Matuku-takotako / Sumner Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":-43.567882,"lo":172.760485,"d":5298,"wd":"Q49393209"},{"n":"Rapanui / Shag Rock","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.563072,"lo":172.750358,"d":5327,"wp":"en:Rapanui Rock","wd":"Q7293859","ds":"Shag Rock (also known as Rapanui) is a sea stack that marks the entrance of the Avon Heathcote Estuary near Christchurch, New Zealand. It is a prominent landmark for navigators at sea as well as travelers on the road to Sumner. The rock stands close to the southern shore just above the low tide level.","wpu":"Rapanui Rock","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Shag_Rock.JPG/960px-Shag_Rock.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cave Rock","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":-43.566184,"lo":172.759961,"d":5426,"wd":"Q97654597"},{"n":"seat","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.604601,"lo":172.651432,"d":5603},{"n":"seat","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":-43.604485,"lo":172.649803,"d":5735},{"n":"Battery Observation Post III","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":-43.59184,"lo":172.79171,"d":5907},{"n":"FreshChoice","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":-43.602858,"lo":172.721529,"d":352,"h":"Mo-Su 07:00-21:00","w":"https://lyttelton.store.freshchoice.co.nz/","ph":"+64 3 328 7368","ad":"17 London Street"},{"n":"Lyttelton Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":-43.60281,"lo":172.721355,"d":355,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-17:30, Sa 09:30-13:00","w":"https://www.lhba.co.nz/lyttelton-pharmacy","ad":"19 London Street"},{"n":"Lyttleton Farmers Market","g":"essentials","k":"marketplace","la":-43.602658,"lo":172.721365,"d":372,"h":"Sa 10:00-13:00","ad":"London Street"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":-43.602618,"lo":172.721658,"d":379,"h":"24/7","f":["wc"]},{"n":"Bank of New Zealand","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":-43.602086,"lo":172.719684,"d":447},{"n":"Four Square","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":-43.628392,"lo":172.724461,"d":2505},{"n":"St Saviour's Chapel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.601833,"lo":172.721559,"d":465,"ds":"St Saviour's at Holy Trinity is an Anglican church in Lyttelton, Christchurch, New Zealand. St Saviour's Chapel was relocated from West Lyttelton to Christchurch's Cathedral Grammar School in the 1970s. Following the earthquakes and the demolition of Holy Trinity Church, Lyttelton, St Saviour's was returned to Lyttelton to the site of Holy Trinity in 2013.","wpu":"St Saviour's Chapel","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/St_Saviour%27s_Anglican_Church_90.JPG/960px-St_Saviour%27s_Anglican_Church_90.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lyttelton Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.615,"lo":172.73,"d":1235,"ds":"Lyttelton Harbour / Whakaraupō is a major inlet on the northwest side of Banks Peninsula, on the coast of Canterbury, New Zealand; the other major inlet is Akaroa Harbour, which enters from the southern side of the peninsula.","wpu":"Lyttelton Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Lyttelton_Harbour_206.JPG/960px-Lyttelton_Harbour_206.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Battery Point, Lyttelton","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.602778,"lo":172.740278,"d":1593,"ds":"Battery Point was the site of a coastal defence fort in the Canterbury Region, New Zealand. Located to the east of the Port of Lyttelton, the fort was constructed in 1885 in response to one of the 'Russian scares'. It was also used in the First World War as well as the Second World War.","wpu":"Battery Point, Lyttelton","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Lyttelton_Harbour%2C_Christchurch.jpg/960px-Lyttelton_Harbour%2C_Christchurch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Canterbury Pioneer Women's Memorial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.593556,"lo":172.708139,"d":1728,"ds":"The Canterbury Pioneer Women's Memorial commemorates the pioneer women of Canterbury in New Zealand. After arrival by sea in Lyttelton Harbour, early settlers had to cross the Port Hills via the Bridle Path to reach Christchurch or the Canterbury Plains. The memorial was unveiled at the summit of the Bridle Path in 1940, 90 years after the First Four Ships had arrived in Lyttelton.","wpu":"Canterbury Pioneer Women's Memorial","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Canterbury_Pioneer_Women%27s_Memorial_%28night_shot%29.jpg/960px-Canterbury_Pioneer_Women%27s_Memorial_%28night_shot%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Diamond Harbour, New Zealand","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.628778,"lo":172.731556,"d":2671,"ds":"Diamond Harbour is a small town on Banks Peninsula, in Canterbury, New Zealand. It is on the peninsula's northern coast, on the southern shores of Lyttelton Harbour, and is administratively part of the city of Christchurch.","wpu":"Diamond Harbour, New Zealand","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Diamond_harbour_wharf_2024.jpg/960px-Diamond_harbour_wharf_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Te Tihi-o-Kahukura / Castle Rock","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.586944,"lo":172.698889,"d":2767,"ds":"Te Tihi-o-Kahukura / Castle Rock is a prominent rock outcrop in the Port Hills above Christchurch, New Zealand. It is a short distance north of Te Moenga-o-Wheke / The Tors, on the northern side of Summit Road. It sits directly to the west of the Bridle Path and the Lyttelton road tunnel.","wpu":"Te Tihi-o-Kahukura / Castle Rock","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Te_Tihi-o-Kahukura_%28Castle_Rock%29.jpg/960px-Te_Tihi-o-Kahukura_%28Castle_Rock%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stoddart Cottage","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.626428,"lo":172.741214,"d":2794,"ds":"Stoddart Cottage is located in Diamond Harbour, New Zealand. Built in time for the wedding of his owner, Mark Stoddart, in February 1862, it was the birthplace of artist Margaret Stoddart. Stoddart Cottage is the oldest building in Diamond Harbour.","wpu":"Stoddart Cottage","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Stoddarts_Cottage.jpg/960px-Stoddarts_Cottage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rāpaki","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.604,"lo":172.6795,"d":3348,"ds":"Te Rāpaki-o-Te Rakiwhakaputa, commonly known as Rāpaki or Rapaki, is a small settlement within the Lyttelton Harbour basin. Rāpaki is one of four Banks Peninsula rūnanga (communities) based around marae (tribal meeting grounds). The Rāpaki Marae, also known as Te Wheke Marae, is a meeting ground of Ngāi Tahu and its Hapū o Ngāti Wheke branch.","wpu":"Rāpaki","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/R%C4%81paki_%E2%80%93_Un_petit_paradis_avec_aucun_touriste.jpg/960px-R%C4%81paki_%E2%80%93_Un_petit_paradis_avec_aucun_touriste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"King Billy Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.635,"lo":172.6857,"d":4297,"ds":"King Billy Island (Māori: Aua; officially Aua / King Billy Island) is a small island and Scenic Reserve in Lyttelton Harbour / Whakaraupō on Banks Peninsula, New Zealand.","wpu":"King Billy Island","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Quail_Island_at_low_tide.jpg/960px-Quail_Island_at_low_tide.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Charteris Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-43.644444,"lo":172.698611,"d":4639,"ds":"Charteris Bay is a small settlement on Banks Peninsula in New Zealand. The settlement is located on the south eastern side of Te Wharau / Charteris Bay, a large inlet of Lyttelton Harbour / Whakaraupō. The settlement sits between Diamond Harbour to the east and Teddington to the west.","wpu":"Charteris Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Optimist_boat_shed_at_Charteris_Bay.jpg/960px-Optimist_boat_shed_at_Charteris_Bay.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Christchurch ( ), also known by its Māori name Ōtautahi, is the largest city in the South Island and the second-largest city by urban area population in New Zealand. Christchurch has an urban population of 407,800, and a metropolitan population of 556,500. It is located in the Canterbury Region, near the centre of the east coast of the South Island, east of the Canterbury Plains.","wpu":"Christchurch","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/The_%22Brill%22_Tram_178.New_Regent_St_Christchurch._%2811510530335%29.jpg/960px-The_%22Brill%22_Tram_178.New_Regent_St_Christchurch._%2811510530335%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}